We have lots of experience, not only building AI agents, but also operationalizing this. In fact, 24 months ago, we released Raiden, our first AI agent. We've learned a lot operationalizing LLM, AI agents in particular, because initial reaction from the market was they were using it, even though it had autonomy,

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:22.000
an autonomous capability, a level five capability, people were using that as a chatbot. And over time, it evolved to the extent to now where not only can they identify where are the prompts failing for LLMs, for example, and what are the prompts, how the prompts need to be fixed.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:39.000
Not only we have solved the token economics problem, like months and months, quarters ago, many quarters ago before anyone was talking about it. And we have customers like AMD, Stanley Black and Decker, who are using it at massive scale for data cost optimization and performance.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:10:04.000
For me, if I think one last comment I wanted to make about agents is I think of agents as smart teammates. Everyone is focusing on making agents smarter, that's important. But I frankly think it's a little bit easier. But making the agents or adopting or adapting them as your teammates, I think that's a critical operational challenge is what we are seeing from our customer base.

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:07.000
I'm glad to be part of this panel.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:12.000
Thank you. And I think Paul, you're the only person we haven't heard from yet.

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:26.000
All right. Hi, everyone. My name is Paul St. Phil. I'm a field CTO for AI here at Rubrik. My main charter is helping organizations design and implement their AI resiliency strategy.

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:48.000
So the AI resilience strategy is basically saying I have AI powered business processes that I need to keep trustworthy and operational in scenarios where they're attacked, they fail, or it's just kind of like unexpected behavior. So that's kind of what I do day to day. Happy to be on the line and be a part of the panel.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:11:03.000
Amazing. So we've got some excellent folks to help us learn more about agentic AI. And we'll start off with talking about automation to agency.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:21.000
So many organizations are moving from scripted automation to systems that can reason, plan, and take multi-step actions. Architecturally, what distinguishes a truly agentic system from traditional AI pipelines or workflow automation?

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:35.000
Anyone can jump in here. But what would be your thoughts for the guests here about how that actually changes the picture of where we are today from where we were yesterday?

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:59.000
I can jump in. I look at it very simply. So for us, and my viewpoint is that automation is a tool. It's a power tool. And in contrast, I look at an agent as a teammate, my teammate, my colleague's teammate, or our teammate.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:15.000
So the difference really where it comes to is, are you looking at static, predetermined workflows, whether it is ML pipelines, AI pipelines, or static data pipelines, or even sometimes even dynamic pipelines for that matter?

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:40.000
Whereas agents have the ability to not only, if empowered, and if they truly have the right level of autonomy or agency, as we call it these days, do they really have the ability to follow a plan or even reason with the plan, adjust the plan, redo the goal as relevant, and make the relevant judgment?

00:12:41.000 --> 00:13:05.000
So that's how I look at automation. Automation is foundational. We are all leveraging that. That's mundane, pretty easy stuff if you've done that. And there are very few unknowns. You're essentially executing known-knowns, whereas when it comes to agents, it's the judgment aspect, the reasoning aspect makes it a little bit non-deterministic.

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:27.000
I love the way that you pull out that piece that these agents work alongside you, that we need to leverage them as part of the team. That's so different than the way that we have looked at advanced analytics and AI, in my opinion, up until the last maybe six months to a year.

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:45.000
So we've looked at it differently, and now we're really starting to focus on, hey, how do I leverage these agents in a way that makes it seem like I've got another person sitting beside me helping me out day to day? Anyone else have anything they'd like to add?

00:13:46.000 --> 00:14:04.000
Yeah, I'll jump in with just, there's the React pattern, and then there's also the dynamic enrichment of context, like enabling skills and tools so that the agents, just like a co-worker, can go out and read your guides and see what's available.

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:18.000
I've seen a bunch of concern about the lethal trifecta. I almost think you go backwards where you make it more like an automation when you don't trust it to actually use tools and have access to data in your environment.

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:35.000
And then I think in the near-term future, if we're thinking about assistants and digital co-workers like Aresh just described, I think long-term memory is going to also be key to it, where you're going to want persistence across sessions, and then that's going to be a part of defining what feels agentic.

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:43.000
It has memory, it has dynamic access to data that's relevant when it needs it. That'll make it agentic in my mind.

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:46.000
Amazing.

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:58.000
Taking those two points forward is because if you see a traditional automation, it follows, like was discussed, it follows a predetermined path. If you do X, Y has to happen.

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:11.000
Whereas we are flipping the script with agentic, where you are reasoning it towards a goal, where this agentic AI is planning its own steps, it has the capability to adapt mid-executions.

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:19.000
So it's planning the steps rather than what the developer here has hard-coded where we have in the automation, which is previously done.

00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:26.000
It has the capability to invoke tools and take real actions even in the mid-execution phase.

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:36.000
So it just does not return an answer. It develops this memory, and like Paul said, this memory goes across multiple sessions. It doesn't just stay there.

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:45.000
So the moment an agentic AI system decides to act, this governance becomes a design time conversation.

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:50.000
Stopped being a design time, I would say, and becomes a runtime one.

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:57.000
So this is what truly distinguishes. One where you have hard-coded the rules and it cannot think out of that box tool.

00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:05.000
And one where it has the capability to build its own rules and adapt based on the user input and the output it is expected.

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:07.000
Yes.

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:12.000
All right. Love it. Anything else on that topic?

00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:16.000
Well, you bring up governance. Oh, I'm sorry. Go ahead, Sarah.

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:24.000
Just to say quickly, I definitely agree with everything that people said so far. I tend to think of it as like it's more

00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:29.000
you're making a goal-directed system instead of an instruction-directed system.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:43.000
So you can be a little bit broader with the aims of the entire agentic system than maybe you could if you were, like was just said, like you're stuck in the sort of design phase, then it needs to implement the plan.

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:54.000
The other thing that I think has really changed is that like the systems we're building have changed, but also the way that people are building them has really fundamentally changed with coding agents.

00:16:55.000 --> 00:17:03.000
And I think that sort of opens us up to all of these new different interaction pairing where like it's been discussed.

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:17.000
You might think of these agents as like a teammate, but they're not people, you know, so we have to sort of think about how the human-agent interaction is going to work, both when you're building the systems and then for the end users of the systems.

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:23.000
And I think that opens up a lot of interesting questions and design problems.

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:41.000
Thank you, Sarah. So speaking of questions, there is one question in the question section here that says, Paul spoke about the importance of giving the agent the long-term memory. Won't the performance take a hit in that case?

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:46.000
Anyone?

00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:58.000
Yeah, I think. So one thing I'll say is that I, that's why I kind of left it as saying maybe it's a future thing. Like today, I judge it just based on access to tools.

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:08.000
It may, but I do think like this is the trade-off that we're all playing. Like we're all trying to manage performance. We're all trying to manage what that context window has.

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:25.000
So yes, but I think like the loop engineering, graph engineering, context engineering, it's all the same game. Like we're trying to manage how much can we get out of an agent from a productivity perspective versus the impact that it's having on performance and resources.

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:41.000
Yeah, if I can jump in there, Francis, I absolutely agree with Paul. It's a great question, by the way. Because I think if we believe in static systems versus learning and dynamic systems, memory is critical.

00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:55.000
Not only for tools, but I mean, humans, we have learned over time, right? All the way from before the child has been born itself, the child is learning. And now here we are, we are doing a five perfect strangers thing.

00:18:56.000 --> 00:19:15.000
We have learned a lot over the time. So I think it's critical to have the trade-off comes in, are you giving the right results? If the results are merit, perhaps a little bit of a hit in performance or what people would call latency, I know Francis, he may address this, I'm all for it.

00:19:16.000 --> 00:19:30.000
Are you giving the accurate results with the higher degree of confidence versus giving AI slop kind of answer or a hallucinated answer? I would always take a slight performance hit on that.

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:53.000
Thank you. All right. So we mentioned governance. And so let's talk a little bit about governance for autonomous systems. So as AI agents begin operating across tools, we've talked about that as well, APIs and business processes, what does a practical governance framework look like?

00:19:54.000 --> 00:20:16.000
How should organizations think about permissioning policies, policy layers and boundaries for agent behavior? So how do we corral and ensure that, as you said, we don't get the crazy hallucinations? What are we looking for as far as ensuring that what we're getting out of our agents is something that is useful for business day to day?

00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:37.000
So practically speaking, an agent needs to have three layers. One is it should have the right scoping. Number two, it should be by default, least privileged permissioning per agent, rather than having a shared service account. And most importantly, the policy layer should sit outside the agent's own reasoning.

00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:57.000
So if it tries to do a bad action, in case the model judgment fails, it stopped there and there. And most importantly, you should have a tier level of approval. Now, how would you say what do you mean by a tier level approval is low risk actions, you can give them full approval.

00:20:57.000 --> 00:21:19.000
But for anything that is non-reversible, something that has got to do with personal data, monetary thing or external communication, a human in the loop is required. So start by giving the least privileges, make sure the policies outside the agent's understanding. And if possible, for any touch points, have a human in the loop.

00:21:19.000 --> 00:21:43.000
So these are the three ways I would look at it. Because these agentic AIs, they may work in background, but they do need a human oversight. And this is mirroring how organizations work. A finance analyst can only approve up to a certain limit. Anything over that limit, you need a finance manager. Same way for an agentic, we have to create those rules, we are giving the autonomy there.

00:21:44.000 --> 00:22:03.000
But the governance aspect that we have for human beings has to be replicated for the agentic AI. As we discussed in our previous conversation a couple of minutes back, they are like a friend, an assistant, a coach, and an executive assistant working with humans simultaneously. So the same policy and the governance rule ideally should go there.

00:22:05.000 --> 00:22:06.000
Awesome answer there.

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:30.000
So speaking of designing those guardrails, let's talk about dynamic guardrails. So we talked a little bit about the static guardrails, they often break down when agents adopt or plan across multiple steps. So what techniques or architectural patterns allow governance policies to adapt dynamically as agents reason through tasks?

00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:51.000
I think it's been a little bit spoken to just now, which was the idea that you want something governing the agent that sits outside of its reasoning. I'm a fan of the LLM as a judge-esque approach, like you use AI to govern AI. The main challenge being that most of these things are non-deterministic.

00:22:52.000 --> 00:23:02.000
If in the beginning, we're keeping true to what you said, which is what you want your agents to have agency, then that means that what you're watching for is what's the intent of what it's doing.

00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:29.000
There are some deterministic controls or deterministic watch guards that you want that are DLP-esque. This type of information should never even traverse the prompt. But I do think that when you get into these higher to Souris point goal operated functions, it gets into this place of like, I have to try to understand the intent of what the agents reasoning through and trying to accomplish to then be able to keep pace with it and control it.

00:23:33.000 --> 00:23:57.000
Yeah, so I absolutely agree with that. And by the way, it's interlinking those two questions was spot on. And Paul, I appreciate your context there. So why do we need dynamic guardrails, right? Because we talked about automation, which is pretty static in terms of there's no change in policies or rules as things. It's all predetermined like we discussed.

00:23:58.000 --> 00:24:17.000
What we have learned over time from a Revify perspective, and even my perspective, is there are a bunch of sequences or actions that an agent may take. Individually, if you look at each of those actions, they look pretty reasonable. And you may say, okay, there's low risk, I'll approve.

00:24:17.000 --> 00:24:45.000
But when you stitch all of them together, and with the ability to adapt and reason, all bets are off. And I think that's where one of the approaches we have done with RAID and our agent is we actually say, what are your plans? How would you go about essentially, we are reasoning with the agent to say, list out what are the steps you're going to take in this scenario? We cannot obviously cover all of them. We start with that approach.

00:24:45.000 --> 00:25:09.000
Think of this as engaging a non-deterministic agent to some extent, and trying to give us deterministic answers. So that's something that we repeatedly do. And I know we may talk about human in the loop. So we start with that sort of reasoning with an agent first to understand how extensive does it go.

00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:34.000
And then we also look at what we have learned is cost and optimization have become big issues. I mean, everyone is talking about token economics or clamping down on spend and things like that. So we look at what's the plan, what's the finops or cost governance implication, and then trying to understand the context.

00:25:35.000 --> 00:25:54.000
And we kind of write that in. Think of this as writing into your markdown file, for example. You don't want to push a significant update on Friday 5 p.m. before going for a long weekend. So these are the things we engage with the agent upfront so that there are no broken rules to the extent possible.

00:25:54.000 --> 00:26:07.000
So so far, we and our customers haven't run into issues. There are dynamic guardrails, but we try to get the agent to spell it out in advance to the extent possible.

00:26:10.000 --> 00:26:15.000
Awesome. Very thorough answer there. You had something to add, Sarah?

00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:35.000
Yeah, sorry. I didn't mean to talk over you. I think the auto mode story is a really good example of a dynamic guardrail. So auto mode is something they have in cloud code and other coding agents or sort of like what Paul was saying. Part of the approval process is done by an LLM.

00:26:36.000 --> 00:26:52.000
So you might have your static guardrails written down, but the part of the mode is also another separate model assessing the risk of a tool request, like some code that has been proposed to be run.

00:26:52.000 --> 00:27:08.000
Something that I found interesting was a couple days ago, I think Anthropic made auto mode the default, partially because they found that in practice, it was actually safer than just showing the human the tool request every time.

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:31.000
So they found that I think humans were able to flag like 15% of risky actions, but the auto mode flagged 90% or something. I think this is a good example of like, it might seem riskier to do something like hand a guardrail to another LLM to enforce.

00:27:32.000 --> 00:27:44.000
But it's not always enough to just show humans the information and like expect them to be able to verify or review because like in practice, they might not actually be doing that review.

00:27:45.000 --> 00:27:58.000
I think like human, human in the loop, definitely, we definitely need humans in the loop. It sounds great. But like if in practice, people are just hitting allow every time, which is what they found was happening, the guardrail doesn't end up being enforced.

00:27:59.000 --> 00:28:03.000
So I think there's, yeah, again, there's like lots of sort of interesting problems in that space.

00:28:04.000 --> 00:28:09.000
I think I would take that further. So rather than human in the loop, you could have a policy document.

00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:23.000
Now when the agent is executing every time it checks against that policy document. So for example, in the case of accounts payable, you may want to have a policy document for checking whether this payment should go or not.

00:28:23.000 --> 00:28:52.000
So that's how you would probably I agree that human in the loop at every instance will not work, but validating it against a policy document, which can be taken as a set of rules can be one way of ensuring that, you know, and at the same time, it is auditable also, because if you have the agent action telling that I took this decision based on the policy document of the organization, automatically, by default, you're bringing you're bringing the auditability built in.

00:28:53.000 --> 00:29:04.000
So rather than focusing why this happened, and why did the agent do this stuff, you are giving it a grounding saying that this is my policy. This is how we operate. And so I mean, just taking that answer further.

00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:27.000
Awesome. Just want to make sure that we we do have a couple of questions in the chat. The first one when agents work as a team using MCP, what does good observability look like for tracing decisions, handoffs and tool calls end to end?

00:29:27.000 --> 00:29:56.000
Yeah, I don't I don't have an answer for it. But I do think like, what I'm seeing become more and more popular now is the idea of agentic identities, like, agent, identity space with agents is like a big one, lots of different views of it. But what I'm hearing a lot of popularity around is the idea that like, give your agents identities. And then when they're doing an operation, most likely it's on behalf of a human or it has some type of approval.

00:29:56.000 --> 00:30:22.000
flow, where now it's going to be mint, minted a scoped token. So now we know that the agent is doing something, it has restricted privileges for only a specific point in time. And then you want to layer observability on top of that. Because usually what happens is bringing an observability with no sense of control over what's happening, like the juice isn't worth the squeeze. So I think the idea is what's the end to the means here, you're trying to observe so that you can restrict and scope.

00:30:22.000 --> 00:30:51.000
Yeah, so thanks for asking the question. So I'll try to not talk about River five, we do offer AI observability and AI FinOps. So what we learned was, I'll tell you a little bit of by how we ended up here. What we noticed was data platform usage. Last year was speaking, picking up, picking up as more and more LLM scope.

00:30:53.000 --> 00:31:13.000
And it was a complete disconnect. People were even before token economics became an issue, that data spend on the Google Cloud data, BigQuery, Snowflakes, Databricks were continuously increasing. What we realized was the impact of the compute on running the AI workloads was not quantified at all.

00:31:13.000 --> 00:31:37.000
So when to answer the question of head on, we look at observability as end to end, all the way from who posts, it could come from an application, or it could come from a direct prompt, what is the workload on the LLMs, the token usage, who's the user, is it an agent, or is it a human, which department attribution to show back and, and, and chargeback models.

00:31:37.737 --> 00:31:38.737
What is an agent?

00:31:38.737 --> 00:31:40.217
Or is it a human?

00:31:40.217 --> 00:31:41.217
Which department?

00:31:41.217 --> 00:31:46.017
Attribution to showback and chargeback models?

00:31:46.017 --> 00:31:47.057
Is the prompt failing?

00:31:47.057 --> 00:31:48.857
How much time is it taking?

00:31:48.857 --> 00:31:50.917
Can you optimize the prompt?

00:31:50.917 --> 00:31:53.137
And also doing tracing, end-to-end tracing.

00:31:53.137 --> 00:31:55.937
We talked about function calls earlier, right?

00:31:55.937 --> 00:32:01.376
Let's say the first call, first prompt took 10 function calls.

00:32:01.376 --> 00:32:04.696
