So understanding that, bringing the concept of tracing to observability, particularly

00:32:10.617 --> 00:32:13.577
in the agentic context, is critical.

00:32:13.577 --> 00:32:18.777
So that you are at least trying to take a non-deterministic infrastructure and try to

00:32:18.777 --> 00:32:27.016
get closer to the determinism which folks in IT data and AI are getting used to now.

00:32:27.016 --> 00:32:33.176
So to put what these two gentlemen said in a much more simple language is, observability

00:32:33.176 --> 00:32:36.417
in an agentic system is like a flight recorder.

00:32:36.417 --> 00:32:41.257
You are not reconstructing what happened, but you are reconstructing why that decision

00:32:41.257 --> 00:32:42.777
was made.

00:32:42.777 --> 00:32:45.177
And that's how I would look at observability.

00:32:45.177 --> 00:32:50.097
Because what we are thinking observability is a log file, log file tells you, I called

00:32:50.097 --> 00:32:55.737
this API agentic, I called this API, I went into this database, pulled this data out,

00:32:55.737 --> 00:32:57.256
and I did this decision.

00:32:57.256 --> 00:32:58.976
It doesn't tell you why it did it.

00:32:58.976 --> 00:33:02.537
But whereas if you look at it from a perspective of flight recorder, when you look at all the

00:33:02.897 --> 00:33:07.256
data points together, you understand why the pilot made that decision based on what factor.

00:33:07.256 --> 00:33:11.696
So I would say, think of it as a flight recorder where you need to understand why the decision

00:33:11.696 --> 00:33:15.817
has been made, so that you can understand what went wrong, what could be done better

00:33:15.817 --> 00:33:17.656
and what really happened.

00:33:17.656 --> 00:33:21.936
So yeah, to put it in very simple terms with what Girish and Paul put forward.

00:33:21.936 --> 00:33:24.016
Yeah, I'll actually take it to the next level.

00:33:24.016 --> 00:33:26.737
