gaps are that you have seen with teams trying to move from a prototype agent into

00:46:57.057 --> 00:47:01.137
enterprise grade deployments, because that's the that's the ultimate goal.

00:47:01.257 --> 00:47:08.296
We it's fun to do all of the pre-work and really dig in deep and understand and build

00:47:08.296 --> 00:47:11.697
knowledge. But the goal is to push to production at some point.

00:47:11.697 --> 00:47:16.896
So can you tell me a little bit and our guests a little bit about what you've seen in

00:47:16.896 --> 00:47:20.817
your own organizations around getting from prototype to production?

00:47:20.817 --> 00:47:27.057
OK, I can go ahead since everyone is white, OK, so, well, actually, you need a couple of

00:47:27.057 --> 00:47:29.896
so rather than governance, I think we've talked a lot about it.

00:47:30.257 --> 00:47:35.817
You need a very good data connect data set to operate on an integration set together.

00:47:36.497 --> 00:47:42.976
I think on agent AI, both they are if you, you know, garbage in, garbage out.

00:47:42.976 --> 00:47:48.536
So if you do not have a rich data set and a very well integration set, you need to

00:47:48.937 --> 00:47:54.057
have that rich set of data and integration set in so that all the scenarios that would

00:47:54.057 --> 00:47:58.776
probably come in your production, you do not want to learn from your mistakes in your

00:47:58.776 --> 00:48:01.817
production system. You want to learn from your mistakes in the sandbox.

00:48:01.817 --> 00:48:03.737
I'm not even talking of development and quality.

00:48:04.257 --> 00:48:08.057
You need to have that rich customer data and very well integrated.

00:48:08.057 --> 00:48:13.497
So how is my, you know, how is my team, how is my team, how is my team, how is my team

00:48:13.577 --> 00:48:17.416
You need to have that rich customer data and very well integrated.

00:48:17.416 --> 00:48:24.296
So how is my sales data, sales table data connected to my customer satisfaction data?

00:48:24.776 --> 00:48:26.057
These two are interrelated.

00:48:26.057 --> 00:48:29.497
If I don't sell well, my customer relationship is problem.

00:48:29.497 --> 00:48:31.336
I'm just putting it at a very simplistic level.

00:48:31.336 --> 00:48:35.817
But you need to find these data pointers and these data sets have to be initially put in

00:48:35.817 --> 00:48:36.937
a sandbox environment.

00:48:36.937 --> 00:48:41.897
And most importantly, how is data is interlinked, is which aspect of my unique key of my

00:48:41.897 --> 00:48:47.336
table integrates sales and customer relationship and then run that AI agent on top of it and

00:48:47.336 --> 00:48:49.737
run it in sandbox exhausted.

00:48:49.737 --> 00:48:54.537
And then you, I think you will see this is the problem because when we are in a sandbox

00:48:54.537 --> 00:48:59.336
data and let's be honest, sandbox databases and systems are not really well maintained.

00:48:59.336 --> 00:49:03.976
They are meant where people try, they do their moonshot ideas and then they leave.

00:49:03.976 --> 00:49:09.257
But I think now has become even more critical where you have these sandbox systems which

00:49:09.257 --> 00:49:15.737
have anonymized production data, making sure that no critical customer data that can uniquely

00:49:15.737 --> 00:49:20.137
identify customer uniquely, you anonymize it and put it in the sandbox and train it.

00:49:20.777 --> 00:49:22.537
And then you bring it out to production.

00:49:22.537 --> 00:49:27.176
And of course, whatever is the standard process of development, quality, and then production.

00:49:27.176 --> 00:49:29.257
So that's how I would put at it.

00:49:29.257 --> 00:49:33.257
So that's the biggest operational gap, making sure you have the right data set and the integration

00:49:33.257 --> 00:49:33.976
of that data set.

00:49:33.976 --> 00:49:34.476
Yep.

00:49:35.257 --> 00:49:41.417
Yeah, definitely agree that like getting the data right is a big part of the process.

00:49:41.417 --> 00:49:48.777
Something that we see is like often, you know, depending on sort of the maturity of the organization

00:49:48.777 --> 00:49:54.216
or their data organization, you might have a lot of code and you have data in the right

00:49:54.216 --> 00:49:59.576
places, but there's a lot of business logic or additional context that lives in the data

00:50:00.377 --> 00:50:04.856
team's heads or lives somewhere that the agent can't access.

00:50:06.056 --> 00:50:11.336
And that can be like a major problem if you're trying to make some kind of agent that does

00:50:11.336 --> 00:50:14.456
something with the data or ingest the data, does analysis on the data.

00:50:15.497 --> 00:50:20.537
Because the tables themselves or even like, you know, minimal data dictionaries often

00:50:20.537 --> 00:50:27.176
can't tell the agent enough information for it to do, you know, joins that make sense

00:50:27.176 --> 00:50:29.096
or analyses that make sense.

00:50:29.176 --> 00:50:35.497
You even things like, you know, you have two columns with the same similar name and your

00:50:35.497 --> 00:50:39.497
data team knows from five years of experience that you always use this one for this case,

00:50:39.497 --> 00:50:41.176
but they don't have that written down anywhere.

00:50:41.176 --> 00:50:42.856
The agent isn't going to know that.

00:50:42.856 --> 00:50:44.936
And then it's going to give you the wrong answer.

00:50:44.936 --> 00:50:50.936
So figuring out where that team wants to, you know, encode that information, getting

00:50:50.936 --> 00:50:53.336
that encoding done successfully.

00:50:53.336 --> 00:50:57.737
And I think also having a good feedback loop for if you have an agent, it's answering

00:50:57.737 --> 00:50:59.257
questions about that data.

00:50:59.257 --> 00:51:01.017
You're encoding the context somewhere.

00:51:01.576 --> 00:51:05.017
You need to know what when questions were answered incorrectly.

00:51:06.056 --> 00:51:09.336
And so that you can improve the context layer with underlying data.

00:51:10.777 --> 00:51:17.497
Another thing we also come across is that when you're doing a prototype, it's often

00:51:17.497 --> 00:51:18.696
make a prototype.

00:51:18.696 --> 00:51:22.216
Everyone who has access to the prototype has the same level of data access.

00:51:22.216 --> 00:51:26.537
When you move it to production, you now have people across the organization who need to

00:51:26.537 --> 00:51:29.576
use the agent and each person has different permissions.

00:51:29.576 --> 00:51:34.297
And so you need to think about, you know, user level scope permissions and which might

00:51:34.297 --> 00:51:38.056
mean that different people are going to get different answers out of your agent.

00:51:38.056 --> 00:51:39.176
And that can be really important.

00:51:40.216 --> 00:51:44.856
Yeah, I think I quickly want to interstitch those two.

00:51:45.576 --> 00:51:51.737
What we have seen is, to your question, Francis, prototypes are there's always this innovation

00:51:51.737 --> 00:51:58.696
lab or team that's tag team that comes together, a proof of concept that everyone is excited

00:51:58.696 --> 00:51:59.417
there.

00:51:59.417 --> 00:52:01.816
And then they declare success.

00:52:01.816 --> 00:52:05.257
The demo set, the data is not representative.

00:52:05.257 --> 00:52:10.537
And then they want to the performance is great in this prototype of proof of concept.

00:52:10.537 --> 00:52:11.897
Then they turn it on.

00:52:11.897 --> 00:52:17.897
Then who's who's going to who's going to who's running point?

00:52:17.897 --> 00:52:18.456
No one.

00:52:18.456 --> 00:52:18.777
Right.

00:52:18.777 --> 00:52:22.537
So is it a chief AI team, chief AI officer?

00:52:22.537 --> 00:52:23.737
Then it becomes organized.

00:52:24.456 --> 00:52:26.777
Literally, what means is ownership and playbook.

00:52:26.777 --> 00:52:30.777
That's what we see in enterprises that sometimes extends the scope.

00:52:30.777 --> 00:52:35.737
And also, is the scale that you got and performance that you got in a prototype?

00:52:35.737 --> 00:52:37.017
Is it representative?

00:52:37.017 --> 00:52:40.056
Maybe it didn't really represent that accurate actual data.

00:52:40.056 --> 00:52:42.137
I think Sarah was alluding to this.

00:52:42.137 --> 00:52:46.216
And the third and most important thing, which we see is no one thinks of cost.

00:52:46.216 --> 00:52:48.777
No one thinks about the tokens being burned.

00:52:49.336 --> 00:52:53.897
Everyone looks at just, hey, I'm spending so much on cloud or chat GPT.

00:52:53.897 --> 00:52:55.336
They ignore the application.

00:52:55.336 --> 00:52:56.456
They ignore the training.

00:52:56.456 --> 00:52:59.737
They ignore the data platform impact as they do.

00:52:59.737 --> 00:53:04.297
Cost is getting to be, as we all know, in the past six months, the number one criteria

00:53:04.297 --> 00:53:05.976
for all AI use cases.

00:53:05.976 --> 00:53:06.537
Right.

00:53:06.537 --> 00:53:09.417
So I think the cost governance is a criticality.

00:53:09.417 --> 00:53:13.336
That's a significant gap we are seeing between prototype and production these days.

